<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cardiganshire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cardiganshire is a historic county on the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardigan-bay">Cardigan Bay</a>, today the county of Ceredigion. It took its name from the early medieval kingdom of Ceredigion, which was joined with Ystrad Tywi to form Seisyllwg, and was made a shire after Edward I's conquest in 1284. Its towns include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberystwyth">Aberystwyth</a> and Cardigan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cardiganshire.jpg" alt="Cardiganshire" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chris Denny · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Melindwr,_Eglwys_Fach_-_geograph.org.uk_-_646101.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
